**Randy (2026-09-30 20:17 PDT):**

Campaign 002 is complete and should remain immutable. The next task is **post-campaign derived inspection tooling only**; do not reopen, retry, replace, or modify any Campaign 002 scored attempt or frozen evidence.

Create a reusable **Synchronized Experiment Replay Playbook**, then execute that playbook immediately on **Campaign 002 Home01** as the reference implementation. The goal is to let me inspect what Robot-Codex/Astra was observably doing over time, not merely see the final evaluator result.

The replay should use the simulation video/timeline as the synchronization spine. When I scrub or jump to any point, I want the rest of the interface to move to the same moment and show, as faithfully as the retained evidence permits:

- the simulation/world view;
- Astra's observable messages, tool calls, tool results, action/authority receipts, observations, handback attempts and controller transitions;
- the filesystem tree as it existed at that point;
- the contents of a selected file at that point, plus useful before/after diffs for file creates/edits;
- timeline markers for meaningful events such as observations, robot actions, file writes, rejected/accepted handback and conventional-controller resumption;
- a fully browsable final filesystem when I jump to the end.

Do **not** attempt to expose or reconstruct hidden chain-of-thought. Show only retained observable behavior and artifacts. Temporal alignment and filesystem reconstruction must be evidence-bound: independently verify the mapping between simulation time, wall/receipt time, model/tool events and file changes. If Campaign 002 did not retain enough information to reconstruct an exact intermediate filesystem state at some moment, make that limitation explicit rather than inventing state. Design the playbook so future experiments can capture any missing provenance prospectively and generate this replay more automatically.

Treat Home01 as the reference case because it contains the rejected first handback, the additional protected setup-B action, the accepted second handback, and conventional follow-through. Build the playbook and the Home01 implementation together, feed implementation lessons back into the playbook, and test the finished replay as an actual interactive artifact—not just static documentation. Preserve source links/provenance so I can inspect the underlying evidence from the replay.

Once the Home01 replay and reusable playbook are complete and verified, stop for review. Do not start the next task-expansion experiment yet. No additional routine approval is needed unless you reach a genuinely new human decision boundary, permission requirement, spending requirement, destructive action, or other constraint already covered by the project instructions.
